Where to Get the Apps:
Generally, it's best to obtain these tools from their official websites or reputable sources to avoid bundled malware or tampered software. Here's a breakdown for the tools you listed:
 * jadx: The primary source is its GitHub repository. You can download pre-built binaries or build it from the source code.
   * Official Link: Search "jadx GitHub"
 * APKTool: You can find the latest version and installation instructions on its official website or GitHub page.
   * Official Link: Search "APKTool website" or "APKTool GitHub"
 * IDA Pro: This is commercial software, so you'll need to purchase a license from Hex-Rays.
   * Official Link: https://www.google.com/search?q=hex-rays.com
 * Ghidra: This is a free tool available directly from the NSA's GitHub repository.
   * Official Link: Search "Ghidra GitHub"
 * Wireshark: Download it from the official Wireshark website. They offer installers for various operating systems.
   * Official Link: wireshark.org
 * Burp Suite: You can download the free "Community Edition" from PortSwigger's official website. The "Professional" version requires a paid license.
   * Official Link: portswigger.net
 * Metasploit Framework: Often comes pre-installed in penetration testing distributions like Kali Linux. You can also install it separately on other Linux distributions or macOS. Rapid7, the developers, provide installation instructions.
   * Official Link: Search "Metasploit Framework installation" or visit rapid7.com
 * Nmap: Download installers for various operating systems from the official Nmap website.
   * Official Link: nmap.org
 * Nikto: Usually included in Kali Linux. It can also be installed on other systems from its source (often found on GitHub or similar repositories).
   * Official Link: Search "Nikto web scanner" to find its repository.
 * OWASP ZAP: Download it from the official OWASP website. They have installers for different platforms.
   * Official Link: owasp.org (search for ZAP)
 * OpenVAS: Often part of larger security distributions like Kali Linux or can be installed separately on Linux systems. Greenbone Networks is the main sponsor.
   * Official Link: greenbone.net
 * Qualys: This is a commercial platform, so you'll need to sign up for their services through their official website.
   * Official Link: https://www.google.com/search?q=qualys.com
 * OpenSSL: This is a library that's usually pre-installed on most Linux and macOS systems. For Windows, you can find official or reputable third-party distributions.
   * Official Link: openssl.org
 * Libsodium: This is a library that can be installed through package managers on various operating systems or built from source.
   * Official Link: libsodium.org
 * Cobalt Strike: This is a commercial red teaming tool that requires a paid license and is typically obtained through authorized channels.
 * Kali Linux: Download the ISO image from the official Kali Linux website. You can then install it on a virtual machine or as a standalone operating system.
   * Official Link: kali.org
 * Security Onion: Download the ISO image from the official Security Onion website. It's designed to be installed as a standalone Linux distribution.
   * Official Link: securityonion.net
 * Snort: Often included in security distributions like Security Onion or can be installed separately on Linux and Windows.
   * Official Link: snort.org
